# ==============================================================================
# Universal .gitignore — drop into any project before the first `git add`
# ==============================================================================
#
# Curated 2026-08-08 from a vault-wide audit. Two rules shaped it:
#
#   1. AGGRESSIVE on secrets and provably-generated output.
#   2. CONSERVATIVE on anything that might be authored content. Over-ignoring
#      hides real work: one project in this vault carried 95,948 insertions of
#      uncommitted backend code that nothing could see. A file wrongly ignored
#      is indistinguishable from a file that does not exist.
#
# Order matters. Broad patterns first, `!` re-includes after — git applies the
# LAST matching rule.
#
# CRITICAL: .gitignore does not untrack what is already committed. If a secret
# was committed before this file existed, adding it here changes nothing — the
# value stays in history and must be ROTATED, then removed with
# `git rm --cached <file>`. Check before trusting this file:
#     git ls-files | grep -E '\.env|\.dev\.vars|\.pem$|\.key$'
#


# ── Secrets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The reason this file exists. Every real .env variant, then example files
# re-included so onboarding still works.

.env
.env.*
.dev.vars
.dev.vars.*
*.local.env

!.env.example
!.env.*.example
!.env.sample
!.env.template
!.dev.vars.example

